WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.200
Movie, relativity, time and space according to Einstein.

00:04.200 --> 00:07.600
Imagine if time could slow down when you move faster,

00:07.600 --> 00:11.100
or if space could bend and warp around massive objects.

00:11.100 --> 00:13.200
Intriguing, isn't it?

00:13.200 --> 00:17.300
Now think about the clock ticking on your wall or the ground beneath your feet.

00:17.300 --> 00:19.800
They seem constant, unchanging.

00:19.800 --> 00:23.700
But what if I told you that's not the entire picture?

00:23.700 --> 00:28.200
Every day we navigate through a world of time and space that seems straightforward.

00:28.300 --> 00:31.500
Yet there's much more to it than meets the eye.

00:31.500 --> 00:34.900
Our understanding of these fundamental elements was dramatically altered

00:34.900 --> 00:37.600
when a certain physicist stepped onto the scene.

00:37.600 --> 00:39.700
His name? Albert Einstein.

00:39.700 --> 00:40.700
His game?

00:40.700 --> 00:43.700
Revolutionizing our perception of reality.

00:43.700 --> 00:48.100
Einstein introduced a radical concept, the theory of relativity,

00:48.100 --> 00:52.200
that challenges our conventional understanding of time and space.

00:52.200 --> 00:56.200
He suggested that time can slow down and space can warp,

00:56.200 --> 00:59.700
a concept that shook the very foundation of physics.

00:59.700 --> 01:02.100
Sounds like science fiction, doesn't it?

01:02.100 --> 01:06.700
But this is exactly what Albert Einstein proposed with his theory of relativity.

01:06.700 --> 01:09.700
So what exactly is this theory of relativity?

01:09.700 --> 01:11.900
Let's break it down.

01:11.900 --> 01:14.900
When you hear the term theory of relativity,

01:14.900 --> 01:18.700
it might sound like something out of a highbrow science fiction novel,

01:18.700 --> 01:22.100
but fear not, it's much simpler than it sounds.

01:22.100 --> 01:23.700
Imagine you're on a train,

01:23.700 --> 01:27.200
and you toss a ball straight up in the air and catch it.

01:27.200 --> 01:30.000
To you, the ball just went up and down.

01:30.000 --> 01:32.600
But to someone watching from outside the train,

01:32.600 --> 01:35.400
the ball took a parabolic path.

01:35.400 --> 01:38.000
That's because even though you're on a moving train,

01:38.000 --> 01:40.800
the laws of physics still work the same for you.

01:40.800 --> 01:43.600
This is the essence of Einstein's first postulate,

01:43.600 --> 01:47.500
the laws of physics are the same for all observers.

01:47.500 --> 01:49.200
Now let's talk about light.

01:49.200 --> 01:52.600
If you've ever driven towards a car with its headlights on,

01:52.600 --> 01:55.700
you might have noticed that the light seems to reach you just as fast

01:55.700 --> 01:59.300
whether you're driving towards the light or away from it.

01:59.300 --> 02:03.000
This is a reflection of Einstein's second postulate.

02:03.000 --> 02:05.500
The speed of light is the same for all observers,

02:05.500 --> 02:07.700
no matter their state of motion.

02:07.700 --> 02:10.100
But here's where things get really interesting.

02:10.100 --> 02:12.200
If the speed of light is the same for everyone,

02:12.200 --> 02:14.300
and the laws of physics are the same for everyone,

02:14.300 --> 02:16.300
what changes?

02:16.300 --> 02:18.600
The answer is time and space.

02:18.600 --> 02:21.100
If you're traveling close to the speed of light,

02:21.100 --> 02:23.400
time will actually slow down for you,

02:23.400 --> 02:25.700
compared to someone who is stationary.

02:25.700 --> 02:27.500
Space will also contract,

02:27.500 --> 02:30.200
making distances seem shorter.

02:30.200 --> 02:34.000
This is known as time dilation and length contraction.

02:34.000 --> 02:35.500
Now you might be wondering,

02:35.500 --> 02:38.100
how does gravity fit into all of this?

02:38.100 --> 02:39.100
Well,

02:39.100 --> 02:41.200
Einstein proposed that mass and energy

02:41.200 --> 02:44.100
actually curve space and time around them.

02:44.100 --> 02:46.200
That's why planets orbit around the sun,

02:46.200 --> 02:48.200
they're following the curve of space and time

02:48.200 --> 02:50.800
created by the sun's mass and energy.

02:50.800 --> 02:51.800
In other words,

02:51.800 --> 02:53.800
Einstein proposed that space and time

02:53.800 --> 02:56.400
are intertwined into a four-dimensional fabric

02:56.400 --> 02:57.800
called space-time,

02:57.800 --> 03:00.000
which is curved by matter and energy.

03:00.000 --> 03:02.100
Now that we have a basic understanding,

03:02.100 --> 03:03.500
let's delve a little deeper

03:03.500 --> 03:06.600
into the key points of Einstein's theory.

03:06.600 --> 03:09.400
At the heart of Einstein's theory of relativity

03:09.400 --> 03:11.600
are three key concepts,

03:11.600 --> 03:13.100
time dilation,

03:13.100 --> 03:14.400
length contraction,

03:14.400 --> 03:16.900
and the equivalence of mass and energy,

03:17.000 --> 03:19.000
famously encapsulated in the equation

03:19.000 --> 03:21.300
E equals McSquared.

03:21.300 --> 03:23.400
Let's start with time dilation.

03:23.400 --> 03:24.600
Imagine you're on a spaceship

03:24.600 --> 03:27.000
traveling close to the speed of light.

03:27.000 --> 03:28.200
According to relativity,

03:28.200 --> 03:29.800
time for you would pass more slowly

03:29.800 --> 03:32.600
compared to someone stationary on Earth.

03:32.600 --> 03:35.400
This isn't a mere illusion or trickery of the mind,

03:35.400 --> 03:38.000
but a literal slowing down of time.

03:38.000 --> 03:40.200
This is what we call time dilation,

03:40.200 --> 03:41.800
and it's one of the most fascinating

03:41.800 --> 03:44.000
aspects of relativity.

03:44.000 --> 03:46.300
Next, we have length contraction.

03:46.300 --> 03:47.600
This is the idea that,

03:47.600 --> 03:50.400
from the perspective of a stationary observer,

03:50.400 --> 03:52.500
an object in motion will appear shorter

03:52.500 --> 03:54.900
in the direction of motion.

03:54.900 --> 03:56.600
Just like time dilation,

03:56.600 --> 03:58.700
this isn't an optical illusion,

03:58.700 --> 04:00.900
but a genuine physical effect.

04:00.900 --> 04:03.700
So if you were to race past Earth in your spaceship,

04:03.700 --> 04:06.100
not only would time slow down for you,

04:06.100 --> 04:08.300
but your spaceship would also appear shorter

04:08.300 --> 04:10.900
to someone watching from Earth.

04:10.900 --> 04:12.400
Finally, let's talk about

04:12.400 --> 04:15.300
the equivalence of mass and energy.

04:15.300 --> 04:17.800
This is the principle behind the famous equation

04:17.800 --> 04:20.600
E equals mc squared.

04:20.600 --> 04:23.000
What it means is that mass and energy

04:23.000 --> 04:24.700
are interchangeable.

04:24.700 --> 04:27.800
They're two sides of the same coin.

04:27.800 --> 04:29.700
This principle is at the heart of everything

04:29.700 --> 04:32.500
from nuclear power to the workings of the sun.

04:32.500 --> 04:35.200
But what makes these concepts truly incredible

04:35.200 --> 04:37.200
is that they're not just theoretical,

04:37.200 --> 04:39.600
they've been confirmed by countless experiments

04:39.600 --> 04:40.800
and observations,

04:40.800 --> 04:43.500
from the bending of starlight around the sun,

04:43.500 --> 04:47.000
to the operation of GPS satellites.

04:47.000 --> 04:48.400
So there you have it,

04:48.400 --> 04:50.400
a brief overview of Einstein's

04:50.400 --> 04:53.100
revolutionary theory of relativity,

04:53.100 --> 04:55.400
a theory that has completely transformed

04:55.400 --> 04:58.200
our understanding of the universe.

04:58.200 --> 05:00.200
Einstein's theory is more than just

05:00.200 --> 05:02.100
a scientific curiosity,

05:02.100 --> 05:05.500
it has profound implications for our daily lives.

05:05.500 --> 05:07.400
You see, his relativity theory

05:07.400 --> 05:10.900
is the backbone of modern GPS navigation.

05:10.900 --> 05:12.400
Without Einstein's understanding

05:12.400 --> 05:14.500
of how gravity affects time,

05:14.500 --> 05:16.100
GPS coordinates would drift

05:16.100 --> 05:18.300
by several kilometers each day.

05:18.300 --> 05:20.000
It's astounding how a theory,

05:20.000 --> 05:22.000
born from scribbles on a chalkboard,

05:22.000 --> 05:24.400
now guides us through the world.

05:24.400 --> 05:26.500
So next time you use your phone's GPS,

05:26.500 --> 05:28.900
remember, you're relying on Einstein's

05:28.900 --> 05:30.800
theory of relativity,

05:30.800 --> 05:32.900
a theory that shows us that time and space

05:32.900 --> 05:35.200
are far more fascinating and complex

05:35.200 --> 05:36.800
than we ever could have imagined.

